A||N||9||mafv8||V8 Tach modification||||1||
Z||000000||mafv8||10-10-2001||07:13 PM||||I still have a problem with my tach, I had asked how to convert my tach to read a V8, the awnser was to replace the capacitor with a different value, in this case a 0.0064uF. This I have done but it still reads high, any ideas about what may be wrong ?, the car is an 84  with a 350 V8 and the cluster (a 120mph style) came from a V6,||||reg||1
Z||000001||LS1swap||10-10-2001||07:40 PM||||Does this help at all? I plan on using the instructions in the first link(Go to articles and trouble shooting then tach revers engineering). I am getting close so post us on how it turns out. <A HREF="Http://home.t-online.de/home/O_Scholz/fieroidx.html" TARGET=_blank>Http://home.t-online.de/home/O_Scholz/fieroidx.html</A> <BR>or <A HREF="http://www.gcstation.net/~mpupillo/tach.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gcstation.net/~mpupillo/tach.htm</A> <p>[This message has been edited by LS1swap (edited 10-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||rockcrawl||10-10-2001||08:40 PM||||I've done the resistor mod as outlined on Oliver's page, and it works great. <A HREF="http://home.t-online.de/home/O_Scholz/tach.html" TARGET=_blank>http://home.t-online.de/home/O_Scholz/tach.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||BRIAN CORNELIUS||10-11-2001||03:03 PM||||When you used the 1 MEG variable resister to adjust your gauge what was the final ohm rating that you found that recalibrated the tach for the v-8 use? You had said it was not the .0064 uF capacitor. So I assume you kept the stock capacitor and just soldered in a fixed rate resister after you used the variable resister to find the correct rating. If you give up the exact rating it will save many of us of having to use the variable resister and we can just solder the the correct resistor.||||reg||1
Z||000004||pherder||10-11-2001||03:18 PM||||I think a 210K ohm resister would be the correct one for a V8 IAW Oliver's web page.<P>840K ohms is for the 4 cylinders with DIS<BR>420K ohms is for the 4 cylinders w/o DIS<BR>280K ohms is for the 6 cylinders<P>Since Oliver states that you only have one firing of the DIS coils per rev., the V8s should have 4 per rev. thus the 840K/4=210K.<P>I haven't checked it out yet but this is my guess and would be my starting point.<P>Paul||||reg||3
Z||000005||rockcrawl||10-11-2001||03:41 PM||||I never measured the resistance. I used a multi turn trim-pot like the one in the picture, it was just a spare part I had laying around. I left it in the circuit instead of replacing it with a fixed resistor. A Bourns 500k multi-turn trimmer pot is $1.45 vs about $.10 for a fixed resistor. I'd think it would be worth the extra $1.35 for being able to dial it in perfectly. I need to take the dash apart again, when I do I'll measure the actual resistance. I set mine up to match the tach reading on the Snap-on scanner, so it should be pretty accurate.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mafv8||10-11-2001||05:26 PM||||thanks for the input guys, again I thought I was doing the easier mod by just changing the capacitor. But it sounds like changing the resistor may be just as easy now I know the value, I assume that I should put the old capacitor back before I change the resistor ? and hopefully that will work better.||||reg||1
Z||000007||BRIAN CORNELIUS||10-15-2001||11:46 AM||||I finally converted the tach for v-8 use using Oliver's method. I measured the resistors ohm rating before soldering it in place. It was 161,900 ohms. After reinstalling the instrument pod and starting the engine the tach was only about 100-200 RHM's fast. This may be because I had to solder leads on the variable risistor to reach the adjustment knob while calibrating. Then I removed the leads and soldered the variable resistor in place. Maybe the leads threw it off a little. Has anyone else have the value of their final ohm rating?||||reg||4
Z||000008||rockcrawl||10-16-2001||08:39 PM||||I got a chance to check mine tonight. It's set at 208k, and it's as accurate as I'll ever need it to be. Sounds like Paul was on the right track with his calculation of 210k. 210k would probably be the theoretical exact value.||||reg||7
Z||000009||pherder||10-16-2001||09:09 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rockcrawl:<BR><B>I got a chance to check mine tonight. It's set at 208k, and it's as accurate as I'll ever need it to be. Sounds like Paul was on the right track with his calculation of 210k. 210k would probably be the theoretical exact value.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeaaah!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Got one right <FONT SIZE="1">(for once!)</FONT><P>Be advised ... the fixed resisters have a % to their rating ... a 210K @ 10% could be as low as 189K and as high as 231K throwing off your tach readings.<P>Paul<p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 10-16-2001).]||||reg||1
